    <dependencies>
        <php min-version="8.3"/>
        <!--
        min-version is 32, NOT 28.

        ⚠️ The reason is NOT the one an earlier revision of this comment gave.
        That text said "openregister declares min-version=32"; measured against
        the canonical repo on 2026-08-08, ConductionNL/openregister@development
        declares <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>. It was true when
        written and was undone by openregister#2380. The openconnector#1173 rule
        — min-version must be >= the max of every <app> dependency's floor — is
        therefore satisfied by any value here, and imposes no constraint at all.
        Do not re-derive this floor from openregister's.

        The floor is 32 for two reasons that are true today:

        1. Nothing below 32 is tested, and an untested range is an advertised
           claim we cannot support. .github/workflows/code-quality.yml pins
           nextcloud-test-refs to exactly ["stable32"]; the stable31 leg was
           REMOVED, not merely dropped for coverage.
        2. This app declares <php min-version="8.3"/> directly above. Nextcloud
           28 does not support PHP 8.3, so the pair (NC 28, PHP 8.3) that the
           two lines together advertise is not a configuration that exists.

        max-version stays at 34, which is the fleet-wide value everywhere except
        openconnector (35).
        -->
        <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
        <!-- THE OPENREGISTER REGISTER SLUG NOW MOVES: `procest` -> `dossiq`
             (and the `procest-default` sibling -> `dossiq-default`), together
             with `x-openregister.app`, which for a `type: application`
             configuration IS a register slug rather than an attribution label
             (ImportHandler::autoCreateRegisterIfApplication reads
             `$xOpenregister['app'] ?? $appId` as the slug).

             It moves SAFELY because the rename is a one-column UPDATE on one
             row. Measured, not assumed: an object is bound to its register by
             NUMERIC id — every shard table's `_register` column holds the id,
             and the tables are named
             `oc_openregister_table_<registerId>_<schemaId>` — so no object,
             table, schema link or folder is addressed by the slug and none of
             them has to move. The one thing that must happen first is renaming
             the ROW, because OpenRegister resolves a register by slug and its
             not-found branch CREATES a second, empty register rather than
             failing. `MigrateRegisterSlug` does that, ahead of every step that
             resolves a register, in both blocks below.

             Still frozen, and for the reason the slug is not: the register
             `folder` (`Open Registers/Procest`) is a Files path already on
             disk, and the `procest.parafering.*` / `procest.tenant.*` audit
             action prefixes are already written into OpenRegister's
             append-only audit trail. -->
        <!-- App dependency (not expressible in app-info.xsd): OpenRegister >= 0.2.16
             (MDM engine: x-openregister-quality / x-openregister-dedup schema support and
             on-save qualityScore/qualityStatus materialisation, ADR-045 / OR change
             2026-06-23-mdm-foundation). -->
    </dependencies>

