    <repair-steps>
        <post-migration>
            <!--
                THE TWO Migrate* STEPS CARRY THE `procest` -> `dossiq` APP-ID
                RENAME AND MUST RUN FIRST IN BOTH BLOCKS.

                Nextcloud namespaces both `oc_appconfig` and `oc_preferences` by
                app id. There is no in-place app-id upgrade — the new id is
                simply a different app — so the rename does not move the stored
                rows, it makes every previously stored value unreachable. Every
                reader supplies a default, so nothing errors: the admin settings
                revert to their defaults, the imported register/schema-id
                bookkeeping looks absent, and each user's saved UI state comes
                back blank. Silently.

                ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. MigrateAppConfigKeys must precede
                InitializeSettings (and the register import below it), because
                InitializeSettings writes configuration keys itself. If it ran
                first those keys would already exist under `dossiq` and the
                migration would skip them as "already present", stranding the
                old values under `procest` where nothing reads them.

                Both steps are idempotent, never overwrite a newer value, never
                delete the old rows (so a rollback still finds its config), and
                log-and-continue rather than throwing — see their class
                docblocks for why a throwing repair step is especially bad here.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
            <!--
                MUST run before every step below that resolves a register by
                slug, and before the import InitializeSettings triggers. It
                renames the register ROW (`procest` -> `dossiq`,
                `procest-default` -> `dossiq-default`); the import that follows
                then recognises the register and UPDATES it, instead of taking
                its not-found branch and creating a second, empty one.

                It moves no data. An object is bound to its register by numeric
                id, not by slug, so the rename is one column on one row.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!--
                SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
                OpenRegister resolves the two halves by different keys: a
                REGISTER by slug alone, a SCHEMA by the PAIR
                (application, slug) via findByApplicationAndSlug(). This app
                now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `dossiq`, while every
                schema it already owns still carries `application = procest` —
                so the pair matches nothing and the import's not-found branch
                builds a SECOND, EMPTY schema set while every stored object
                stays bound to the old rows. Measured before this step existed:
                180 schemas under `procest`, zero under `dossiq`.

                Runs alongside MigrateRegisterSlug and before the import.
                Refuses rather than merges where a slug already has a twin
                under the new application id, never deletes a schema, and never
                throws.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
            <!--
                The OTHER half of the same problem, on a different column.
                MigrateRegisterSlug moves `openregister_registers.slug`; this
                moves `openregister_schemas.application`, which nothing else
                touches. Schemas are matched by findByApplicationAndSlug() — the
                PAIR — and their `application` comes from the importing app's own
                Application::APP_ID, so every schema on an instance that predates
                the procest -> dossiq app rename still says `procest` and is
                invisible to the lookup. The import then forks a second, empty
                set of SCHEMAS exactly as it would fork a second register.
                Delegates to OpenRegister's SchemaApplicationMigrator
                (openregister#2811).
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterApplicationId</step>
            <!--
                AHEAD OF THE IMPORT, BECAUSE THE IMPORT CANNOT WORK AROUND A
                FORKED SLUG. `DemoDataService` used to hand the whole mock
                descriptor to OpenRegister under the configuration id
                `dossiq.demo`, and a schema is matched by the PAIR
                (application, slug): every slug missed, and the not-found
                branch is the CREATE branch. One click built 139 duplicate
                schemas with 393 demo objects bound to them, invisible to an
                app that reads its schemas under `dossiq`, and overwrote the
                register's own title with "Dossiq (demo)".

                The import is fixed at source, so this cannot happen again;
                this step is for the instances where it already did. It only
                removes a schema it can prove is the fork (see the class
                docblock for the three conditions), names everything it
                spares, and routes deletion through OpenRegister's audited
                cascade.

                POST-MIGRATION ONLY, for the same reason as RetireOriRegister:
                a fresh install never ran the defective import. The fork can
                only exist on an instance where dossiq was already enabled and
                somebody clicked "install demo data".
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RepairDemoDataSchemaFork</step>
            <!--
                MUST stay first among the SCHEMA steps. OpenRegister's import matches a schema to an
                existing row by SLUG, so a renamed slug in the register JSON
                renames nothing: the import finds no match, creates a SECOND
                schema, and every stored object keeps pointing at the old one —
                data stranded behind a schema nothing reads, which presents as
                an app with no records rather than as an error. Renaming the
                slug on the existing row first makes the import recognise it.
                Ahead of the import trigger, InitializeSettings.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameDutchSchemaSlugs</step>
            <!--
                Before InitializeSettings, and separate from RenameDutchSchemaSlugs:
                this ends a CROSS-APP collision rather than translating a word.
                `supplierInvoice` was claimed by this app and by shillinq, which owns
                the accounts-payable invoice.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameCollidingSchemaSlugs</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
            <!--
                The shipped register data assigns flow steps to Nextcloud
                groups (task-behandelaar -> "behandelaars"). Provision them
                before anything imports or runs that data: the completion
                gate fails closed on a group that does not exist.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\ProvisionAssignedGroups</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\LoadDefaultZgwMappings</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedBezwaarBeroepData</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedBezwaarWorkflowDefinition</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedBesluitvormingTemplates</step>
            <!--
                REPLACES RegisterOriRegister. dossiq no longer declares an ORI
                register (ADR-019/ADR-022: raadsinformatie is decidiq's domain,
                and `ori` was a parallel Dutch-named duplicate of its Popolo
                model). Dropping the declaration stops it being re-created; it
                does nothing about the register already on an existing install,
                which holds real objects — 115 on the reference instance.

                So this step RETIRES the register only once it is empty, and
                warns and keeps it otherwise. Removal never outruns migration.
                Declared in post-migration only: a fresh install never had an
                ORI register to retire.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RetireOriRegister</step>
            <!--
                Post-migration only, for the same reason as RetireOriRegister
                above: a fresh install has no subsidieRegeling objects to move
                onto case types. Running it on install would be a no-op that
                still reports a count, which is the kind of reassuring noise
                that makes a real zero hard to notice.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateSubsidieRegelingToCaseType</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedLhsMatrix</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedVthMatrixCells</step>
            <!--
                VthSeedDataRepairStep SEEDS THE CASE TYPES SeedVthWorkflowTemplates
                RESOLVES, SO IT HAS TO GO FIRST. It shipped the other way round,
                and on a fresh install the lookup could not succeed even once:
                five of the six templates name a caseType slug
                (omgevingsvergunning-bouwactiviteit, toezichtzaak-bouw,
                handhavingszaak twice, klacht-toezicht) that does not exist yet
                when the template step runs. The install reported it as
                "0 seeded, 5 skipped ... not found for template ... (run
                base-register-seed-data first)" and carried on green, so the VTH
                catalogue was simply absent on every new instance.

                The tell was in the message the step prints about itself: a step
                that tells you to run something else first is a step that is
                registered in the wrong place.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\VthSeedDataRepairStep</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedVthWorkflowTemplates</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\CaseFlowSeedDataRepairStep</step>
            <!--
                AFTER EVERY STEP THAT CREATES A CASE TYPE, AND IT USED TO RUN
                BEFORE THEM ALL. This step projects the implicit lifecycle of a
                case type that has no workflow yet into a published "basis"
                template, so what it can project is decided entirely by which
                case types exist when it runs. It sat between
                SeedBezwaarBeroepData and SeedBesluitvormingTemplates, ahead of
                the besluitvorming, VTH and case-flow seeds, and reported its
                own emptiness as success.

                Measured on a clean rig on 2026-09-04: 6 templates after a fresh
                install, 15 after the first `occ maintenance:repair`, stable at
                15 after the second. Nine templates that belong on a new
                instance arrived one pass late, and only because an
                administrator happened to run repair.

                It is registered in <install> for the same reason. The old
                exemption called it a one-way migration of existing rows, which
                it is not: it creates a baseline for every case type the seeds
                above just created, and a fresh install is exactly where they
                are all new.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateWorkflowDefinitions</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\FoldCasePropertiesOntoCase</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedDeadlineMonitoringData</step>
            <!--
                Raises each local bezwaaradviescommissie as a GovernanceBody in
                the decision app and notes the id back on the local row.
                Idempotent twice over: it skips a committee that already carries
                an id, and the seam it dispatches to resolves on
                (sourceApp, externalReference) before writing, so even a lost
                note costs one redundant dispatch rather than a duplicate body.
                Skips cleanly when the decision app is not installed — it is an
                optional runtime dependency and an install without it keeps
                working on its local committees.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateCommitteesToDecidiq</step>
            <!--
                Must run AFTER the register sync that adds the English columns, so it
                can tell the rename case from the back-fill case. Matches BOTH the
                dossiq and dossiq-default registers, which each hold live rows —
                which is why MigrateRegisterSlug renames both siblings and not
                just the first: this step resolves them by slug PREFIX, and a
                half-renamed pair would migrate half the install and say so as
                success. Idempotent, non-destructive, and refuses ambiguous renames.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameDutchDeadlineColumns</step>
            <!--
                Sibling of the step above, but a VALUE migration rather than a
                column one: the `direction` column already has the right name,
                and only the strings inside it move (inkomend/uitgaand/intern ->
                inbound/outbound/internal).

                Scoped to the `direction` COLUMN on purpose. `intern` is also a
                value of the statutory ZGW vertrouwelijkheidaanduiding enum,
                which is exempt from the vocabulary rule; a word-based rewrite
                would have corrupted every confidentiality field in the install.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameDutchDirectionValues</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateArchivalToOpenRegister</step>
            <!--
                A ketenpartner is an organisation, and OpenRegister owns
                Organisation. This moves the rows BEFORE the Partners settings
                page that used to write them is gone, which is why it is a
                repair step and not the occ command it also ships as: an
                administrator who never ran the command would be left with rows
                behind a surface that no longer exists. Idempotent by the
                partner's own uuid, so re-running an upgrade adds nothing.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigratePartnersToOrganisations</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedKccWerkplekData</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\BackfillInformatieobjectMetadata</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\LinkInFlightContractDecisionsRepair</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\LinkInFlightRemainingDecisionsRepair</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedVerwerkingsactiviteiten</step>
            <!--
                Must run AFTER the register sync that adds the English columns, so it
                can tell a rename from a back-fill. Covers every renamed property
                together: a register-scoped step that moved some owners and not others
                would leave the rest silently reading null.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameDutchColumns</step>
            <!--
                Must run AFTER RenameDutchColumns: that step moves the COLUMNS,
                this one moves what is IN them, across every mapped property
                rather than the single `direction` column
                RenameDutchDirectionValues covers. Once the schema declares
                `upheld`, a row still holding `gegrond` does not error — a filter
                on the new value returns null and the caller reads that as
                "nothing to do".

                The ZGW and StUF vocabularies are deliberately NOT in its map:
                they are the standard's, they travel on the wire, and the ZGW
                `vertrouwelijkheidaanduiding` values (openbaar … zeer_geheim) are
                statutory. Translating them would break the mapping the adapter
                exists to perform.

                That paragraph was true of the INTENT and false of the map for
                seventeen values, until dossiq#1841 removed them and
                RealignStatutoryVocabulary below put the rows back.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameDutchValues</step>
            <!--
                dossiq#1596, and it runs immediately AFTER the rename above
                because that is the step whose output it repairs.

                RenameDutchValues translated ONE member of the LHS actorType
                axis (overheid -> government). It could not reach the matrix
                cells, which live inside a JSON column rather than a column of
                their own, so the vocabulary split down the middle: the
                recommendation schema offered `government` while every cell
                said `overheid`. A quarter of the national enforcement strategy
                became unreachable, and it presented as "Geen LHS-cel gevonden"
                — which reads like bad input rather than a broken axis.

                The mapping is removed at source, so a fresh instance cannot
                acquire this. This step is for the instances that already ran
                it, and it is idempotent: a repaired row no longer matches.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RealignLhsActorTypeVocabulary</step>
            <!--
                dossiq#1841, and the same shape as the step above it: the rename
                reached a vocabulary that was never its to translate, and this
                puts the stored rows back.

                Seventeen entries anglicised the ZGW Vertrouwelijkheidaanduiding
                (`confidentiality` on case, caseType, document and documentType;
                `vertrouwelijkheidaanduiding` on informatieobject and
                informatieobjecttype), the StUF message status and the
                zaaksysteem synchronisation status. Every replacement was a value
                the row's own schema REFUSES, so a case handler editing any demo
                case was rejected on a field they had not touched, and
                InformatieobjectAccessGuard — which fails closed on a level it
                cannot rank — treated every `public` document as the most secret
                one the app holds.

                Schema-scoped, unlike the rename: reversing by column would put
                `sent` -> `verzonden` on schemas where `sent` is correct.
                Idempotent — a repaired row no longer matches the filter.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RealignStatutoryVocabulary</step>
            <!--
                Hermiq owns the EU AI Act Art. 14 oversight record now, and
                dossiq's own oversight page is retired. This replays the
                DECISIONS already in dossiq's audit log through the same event
                every new decision travels, so old evidence takes the identical
                path as new evidence and hermiq never reads dossiq's register.
                Idempotent (stable externalRef per source entry) and non-fatal
                (a missing hermiq logs and continues) — an upgrade must not fail
                because an audit projection could not complete, and the local
                copies remain to replay from. POST-MIGRATION only: a fresh
                install has no history to move.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateAiOversightToHermiq</step>
            <!--
                bacAdviceRequest carried the objection id under the name of the
                SCHEMA it $refs (`objectionProceeding`) instead of the property
                the schema declares (`bezwaar`) — so a required property was
                never written, and BezwaarDetail's advice-request widgets, which
                filter on `bezwaar`, showed nothing at all. Idempotent (rows that
                already have `bezwaar` are skipped) and non-fatal.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\BackfillAdviceRequestObjection</step>
            <!--
                One clock: arms an OpenRegister FlowTimer for every in-flight
                TermijnInstance that does not carry one yet (engineTimerId),
                suspending the timer for paused rows. Idempotent, skips
                terminal rows, and never throws — an unreachable engine is a
                warning plus a failed count, not a broken upgrade. Catch-up
                rung fires after migration are absorbed by the
                notificatiesVerstuurd dedup in TermijnTimerFiredListener.
                POST-MIGRATION only: a fresh install has no instances to arm.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\ArmTermijnEngineTimers</step>
            <!--
                LAST, AND A REPORT RATHER THAN AN ACTION — see the identical
                entry in the <install> block below for why it cannot adopt.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\ReportShippedFlowAdoption</step>
        </post-migration>
        <!--
            FRESH INSTALL. Nextcloud does NOT run post-migration on a first install:
            `Installer::installAppLastSteps()` guards both the pre-migration and
            post-migration blocks with `if ($previousVersion !== '')`, and only
            `repair-steps/install` runs unconditionally afterwards. Until this block
            existed a brand-new Dossiq install ran NONE of the steps above — the
            register was never imported and not one of the twelve seed steps ran, so
            a fresh instance had no ZGW mappings, no bezwaar/beroep data, no LHS or
            VTH matrices and no workflow templates.

            Only baseline-CREATING steps are listed. Deliberately EXCLUDED, because
            each operates on data a fresh install does not have:
              RenameDutchDeadlineColumns        rename over existing rows
              RenameDutchDirectionValues        rewrite over existing rows
              RenameDutchColumns                rename over existing rows
              RenameDutchValues                 rewrite over existing rows
              RealignLhsActorTypeVocabulary     repairs rows an earlier rename touched
              MigrateArchivalToOpenRegister     one-way migration
              MigratePartnersToOrganisations    moves existing ketenpartner rows
              MigrateSubsidieRegelingToCaseType moves existing subsidieRegeling rows
              MigrateAiOversightToHermiq        replays existing audit history
              MigrateCommitteesToDecidiq        raises existing committees; none are seeded
              ArmTermijnEngineTimers            arms existing TermijnInstances; none exist yet
              RetireOriRegister                 retires a register a fresh install never had
              FoldCasePropertiesOntoCase        backfill over existing cases
              BackfillInformatieobjectMetadata  backfill over existing documents
              BackfillAdviceRequestObjection    backfill over existing bacAdviceRequests
              LinkInFlight*DecisionsRepair      link IN-FLIGHT decisions; none exist yet
              RepairDemoDataSchemaFork          retires a fork only an already-enabled app could have made

            THE LIST ABOVE IS NOT PROSE. RepairStepRegistrationTest parses this file,
            sweeps every post-migration step, and fails on any step that is neither
            declared in <install> below nor named in its exemption table with a
            reason. ProvisionAssignedGroups shipped registered in post-migration only
            and was invisible to a fresh install for exactly as long as that sweep
            did not exist.
        -->
        <install>
            <!--
                THE SAME TWO Migrate* STEPS, AND THE DUPLICATION IS THE POINT.
                `Installer::installAppLastSteps()` guards BOTH the pre- and
                post-migration blocks with `if ($previousVersion !== '')`; on a
                fresh install `installed_version` is still empty, so neither
                block runs and only `repair-steps/install` executes.

                THIS IS THE PATH THE RENAME ACTUALLY TAKES. Nextcloud discovers
                `dossiq` as a DIFFERENT app from `procest`, so enabling it IS a
                fresh install with an empty `installed_version` — the
                post-migration block above never fires, and the two Migrate*
                steps would never run at all if they were declared only there.
                Declared here they run, and on a genuine upgrade whichever block
                fires first wins while the other is an idempotent no-op.

                They stay ahead of InitializeSettings for the ordering reason
                given in the post-migration block.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
            <!--
                Same reason as in the post-migration block, and the same
                duplication: enabling `dossiq` is a FRESH install as far as
                Nextcloud is concerned, so this is the block that actually runs.
                The register row it renames was created by the previous app id
                and is still there — the rename has to happen here or the import
                below forks a second, empty register.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!--
                SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
                OpenRegister resolves the two halves by different keys: a
                REGISTER by slug alone, a SCHEMA by the PAIR
                (application, slug) via findByApplicationAndSlug(). This app
                now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `dossiq`, while every
                schema it already owns still carries `application = procest` —
                so the pair matches nothing and the import's not-found branch
                builds a SECOND, EMPTY schema set while every stored object
                stays bound to the old rows. Measured before this step existed:
                180 schemas under `procest`, zero under `dossiq`.

                Runs alongside MigrateRegisterSlug and before the import.
                Refuses rather than merges where a slug already has a twin
                under the new application id, never deletes a schema, and never
                throws.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
            <!-- Schema half of the same rename; see the post-migration block. -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterApplicationId</step>
            <!--
                SAME ARGUMENT AS THE THREE STEPS ABOVE, AND IT WAS MISSING.
                Enabling `dossiq` is a fresh install as far as Nextcloud is
                concerned, so this is the block that runs on an instance that
                already holds `procest` schemas under their old Dutch slugs.
                Declared post-migration only, the rename never happened on that
                path and the import forked a second schema set. It must stay
                ahead of InitializeSettings for the reason given there.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameDutchSchemaSlugs</step>
            <!--
                Same as the post-migration block: enabling the app is a fresh
                install as far as Nextcloud is concerned, so an instance that
                already holds a `supplierInvoice` schema reaches the import
                through here. It must stay ahead of InitializeSettings.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\RenameCollidingSchemaSlugs</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
            <!--
                BASELINE-CREATING, AND ONLY A FRESH INSTALL NEEDS IT MOST.
                The shipped case flow assigns its behandelaar step to the
                Nextcloud group `behandelaars`, and membership of a group that
                does not exist is false for everyone — so the completion signal
                is refused fail-closed and the shipped journey cannot be walked
                at all. Declared post-migration only (dossiq#1729), it never ran
                on the one path that had no group to begin with. Ahead of every
                seed below, which is where that data lands.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\ProvisionAssignedGroups</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\LoadDefaultZgwMappings</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedBezwaarBeroepData</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedBezwaarWorkflowDefinition</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedBesluitvormingTemplates</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedLhsMatrix</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedVthMatrixCells</step>
            <!-- SEEDS THE CASE TYPES THE TEMPLATE STEP RESOLVES; see the
                 post-migration block for what the wrong order cost. -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\VthSeedDataRepairStep</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedVthWorkflowTemplates</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\CaseFlowSeedDataRepairStep</step>
            <!-- LAST OF THE SEEDS, because it projects a basis workflow onto
                 every case type the steps above just created. See the
                 post-migration block for what its old position cost. -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\MigrateWorkflowDefinitions</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedDeadlineMonitoringData</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedKccWerkplekData</step>
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\SeedVerwerkingsactiviteiten</step>
            <!--
                LAST, AND A REPORT RATHER THAN AN ACTION. The two flows dossiq
                ships are imported by OpenRegister while the register above is,
                and they arrive `owner = NULL, enabled = false` by that engine's
                design — so nothing runs, and the install says nothing about it.
                This step cannot adopt them (adoption writes the CALLING user's
                uid and an upgrade runs as nobody); it names what is outstanding
                and the command that completes it. It never throws.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Dossiq\Repair\ReportShippedFlowAdoption</step>
        </install>
    </repair-steps>

